<!-- Aura-SOC - rule 100940 (level 0). Domain: suricata,ids,
     Naming convention and load-order trap: see rules/README.md -->
<group name="suricata,ids,">

  <!-- Engine-decode noise, demoted to level 0.

       The native rule 86601 alerts at level 3 on EVERY Suricata alert. On this
       lab that is 28 000+ events per 20 000-line sample, and the overwhelming
       majority are the engine complaining about protocols it could not parse -
       not about an attacker. Measured 2026-08-11 across the five interfaces:

         5142  SURICATA HTTP Request unrecognized authorization method
         3380  SURICATA QUIC failed decrypt
          434  SURICATA SSH invalid banner
          246  SURICATA Applayer (protocol mismatch / wrong direction)
           55  SURICATA STREAM ...
           51  SURICATA HTTP2 too long frame data

       These fire on normal encrypted traffic (QUIC keys the sensor does not
       have, HTTP/2, non-OpenSSH banners). Keeping them at level 3 buries the
       signal and inflates every dashboard count.

       Scope is deliberately narrow: only the `SURICATA ` prefix, i.e. the
       engine's own decoder events (gid 1, sid 22xxxxx / 2xxxxxx internal), and
       only the families listed above. `SURICATA` rules that describe an actual
       protocol abuse are NOT in the list and keep their level. -->

  <rule id="100940" level="0">
    <if_sid>86601</if_sid>
    <field name="alert.signature" type="pcre2">^SURICATA (?:QUIC|HTTP2|STREAM|Applayer|HTTP Request unrecognized authorization method|HTTP unable to match response to request|HTTP Unexpected Request body|SSH invalid banner|NFS|SMB)</field>
    <description>Suricata engine decode noise (not an attack): $(alert.signature)</description>
  </rule>

</group>
